Validation of the beamstrahlung sampling and its shower interface
================================================================

dump.cc       showers an event file twice, with and without degrading the
              beams, and writes one line per event:
                  xbs1 xbs2 x1 x2 eCM sum(E_final) pT(mumu) sum(E_fwd_gamma)
makeplots.py  turns four such files into the validation figure

  # in the Pythia8 examples directory, built like the main itself
  ./dump events.lhe 20000 0 120.0 | grep '^[0-9]' > fcc_plain.dat
  ./dump events.lhe 20000 1 120.0 | grep '^[0-9]' > fcc_degr.dat
  #   ... and the same for an ILC sample into ilc_plain/ilc_degr.dat
  python3 makeplots.py

What each panel establishes:

  A  the sampled x_BS reproduces the CIRCE spectrum the grid was built
     from, over four decades
  B  Pythia's x equals x_LHE/x_BS to 7e-8, the precision of the 11-digit
     LHE momenta: the shower backward-evolves the ISR part only
  C  eCM matches sqrt(x_BS1 x_BS2 s) to 1e-11, and the final-state energy
     sums to (x_BS1+x_BS2)E rather than 2E
  D  ILC 500: the forward-photon energy the shower produces drops by
     8.4 GeV on average, against 9.3 GeV of beamstrahlung energy that left
     with the beam, and the fraction of events with no forward ISR photon
     at all goes from 26% to 43%
  E  FCC-ee 240: the two curves coincide, as they must when only 0.03 GeV
     is removed
  F  the beamstrahlung spectra behind D and E

Note that pT of the hard system is a poor observable here: it is dominated
by genuine hard ISR (radiative return to the Z) and the beamstrahlung shift
does not stand out above that tail.

Comparing generators
====================

compare_sources.py  overlays the same process generated by different codes
                    and draws a ratio to the first sample given:

  python3 compare_sources.py out.pdf \
      "MadEvent (no flavour grouping)=.../unweighted_events.lhe.gz" \
      "MG7 / madspace=.../events.lhe.gz"

It reads 1-x_BS, 1-s'/s and sqrt(shat)/sqrt(s) from the LHE alone, so it
needs no shower, and reports a chi2/ndf per observable.

Two things the script encodes that are easy to get wrong:

  - sqrt(shat) is taken from the final state, not from the incoming
    momenta. Both codes boost the event before writing it and they do not
    pick the same frame, so the beam energies in the two files are not
    comparable; the invariant mass of the final state is.

  - a beam counts as radiating at 1-x_BS > 1e-7, not lower. MadEvent
    writes the tag from Fortran as E15.7 -- 7 significant digits -- so any
    1-x_BS below ~5e-8 is stored as exactly 1, while mg7's "%15.7e" keeps
    8 digits. The tell is a hard edge: 0 MadEvent beams against 74 mg7
    ones in 1e-8..5e-8. Counting at 1e-9 compares mg7's resolution against
    MadEvent's truncation and invents an 8% excess. Nothing physical is at
    stake -- 5e-8 of a 120 GeV beam is 6 micro-eV.

On a checkout older than 28489295f the MadEvent reference must be
generated with `set apply_flavor_grouping False`: before that fix
`dressed_lep` in export_v4.py was decided from the initial states *after*
merged_particles had expanded them, so a plain `e+ e- > mu+ mu-` failed
the test, auto_dsig never called ee_comp_prod, and `pdg2pdf`'s dressed
branch returned only ee_components(1) -- silently dropping the three
beamstrahlung components, 13% of the cross section at fcce240ll, with an
unchanged shat shape. `grep EE_COMP_PROD SubProcesses/P*/auto_dsig1.f`
says whether a given directory has the fix.
